plomatic historians.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-26T11:10:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45807892658", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jerónimo Bécker y González was a Spanish historian, diplomat and journalist.", "newValue": "Jerónimo Bécker y González (1857–1925) was a pivotal Spanish intellectual, diplomat, and historian whose extensive work meticulously documented Spain's colonial past and its foreign relations during a period of profound national introspection following the 1898 disaster. His prolific writings, particularly on Spanish imperial endeavors in Africa and the Americas, established a foundational narrative for understanding Spain's post-colonial identity and its efforts to redefine its place on the global stage amidst declining imperial power.

Bécker's career seamlessly integrated public service with scholarly pursuits; he served in various diplomatic posts, including consul in Morocco and minister plenipotentiary in Norway, experiences that directly informed his historical research. His major works, such as "Historia de las relaciones exteriores de España durante el siglo XIX" (1903) and the multi-volume "Historia política y diplomática de España desde la independencia de los Estados Unidos hasta nuestros días" (1924), offered comprehensive, though often nationalistic, accounts of Spanish foreign policy. He was elected to the prestigious Real Academia de la Historia in 1904, solidifying his authority in Spanish historiography.

